# Frontend structure

Use the **hybrid feature-based** layout (feature folders + shared layer). Match existing folders before inventing new top-level ones.

## Where new code goes

| Layer | Path | Put here |
|-------|------|----------|
| App wiring | `app/` | Router, providers, global app shell |
| Routes | `pages/` | One screen per route; thin — compose features only |
| Feature UI | `features/<name>/components/` | Components for one user flow + co-located `*.module.css` + `__tests__/` |
| Feature state | `features/<name>/hooks/` | Stateful logic for that feature + `__tests__/` |
| Feature API | `features/<name>/services/` | API calls for that feature (use `shared/api/httpClient`) |
| Shared business | `domain/` | Cross-feature types and pure rules (no React, no fetch) |
| Shared infra | `shared/` | `api/`, `styles/`, future generic UI kit |
| Test setup | `test/` | Vitest setup only (jest-dom, cleanup) |

## Rules

- **Pages stay thin** — no business logic or direct `fetch` in `pages/`; delegate to `features/*/hooks` and `features/*/services`.
- **Co-locate styles** — `ComponentName.module.css` next to the component or page; do not add global CSS unless it belongs in `shared/styles/` (tokens, reset).
- **Co-locate tests** — `__tests__/` inside the feature folder, next to the code under test.
- **New user flow** — create `features/<flow-name>/` with `components/`, `hooks/`, `services/` as needed; add a route in `app/routes.tsx` and a page in `pages/`.
- **Avoid** top-level `components/`, `hooks/`, `services/` at `src/` root — that is the old layered layout.

## Data flow

`pages/` → `features/<name>/` (components + hooks) → `features/<name>/services/` or `shared/api/` → `domain/`

## Styling

- Global: `shared/styles/tokens.css`, `shared/styles/reset.css` (imported from `main.tsx`).
- Scoped: `*.module.css` imported as `import styles from './X.module.css'` and `className={styles.foo}`.
